1. Project Overview
Objective:
Build an agentic automation platform focused on intelligent email management. The initial MVP will integrate:

Email MCP: Processes incoming emails, leverages an LLM for natural language understanding and reply generation.

Conductor MCP: Acts as the central orchestrator that performs intent analysis, routes emails to the Email MCP, and coordinates human review.

Dashboard: Provides a user interface for monitoring, feedback, and human oversight on high-risk or complex email cases.

Key Goals:

Automate routine email responses while flagging high-risk cases for human review.

Establish a centralized system that logs every action and provides a foundation for future modules.

Maintain a modular architecture with clear interfaces, ensuring scalability and ease of integration.

2. Phased Development Plan
Phase 0: Preparation & Requirements (Weeks 1-2)
Requirements Gathering:

Finalize the PRD for Email MCP, Conductor MCP, and Dashboard.

Identify functional and non-functional requirements.

Define input/output formats (e.g., JSON schemas) for inter-module communication.

Architecture Design:

Draw high-level architecture diagrams.

Define API endpoints and data flows between modules.

Choose technology stack (programming languages, frameworks, containerization tools).

Team & Environment Setup:

Establish version control, CI/CD pipelines, and a development environment.

Set up container orchestration (e.g., Docker, Kubernetes) for future scalability.

Phase 1: MVP Development (Weeks 3-8)
A. Email MCP Development
Email Ingestion & Parsing:

Implement email reception via SMTP/IMAP.

Parse emails to extract sender, subject, and content.

LLM Integration:

Integrate an LLM for natural language processing.

Develop initial templates and dynamic prompts for generating draft responses.

Risk Assessment & Auto-Response:

Implement logic to differentiate low-risk emails (auto-respond) from high-risk emails (flag for human review).

Testing:

Write unit tests for parsing, LLM integration, and risk assessment.

B. Conductor MCP Development
Intent Analysis & Routing:

Implement the intent analysis module using NLP techniques.

Define routing rules (e.g., if low-risk → forward to Email MCP for auto-response; if high-risk → route back as draft for review).

Logging & Feedback Integration:

Set up comprehensive logging for each decision point.

Implement APIs for receiving feedback from the Dashboard.

Testing:

Write integration tests to ensure proper data flow and routing between the Conductor and Email MCP.

C. Basic Dashboard Setup
User Interface (UI) Development:

Develop a simple dashboard interface that displays:

Incoming email summaries.

Draft replies flagged for human review.

Logs and status of email processing.

Feedback Mechanism:

Enable basic approval/editing functionalities for high-risk emails.

Testing:

Conduct user acceptance testing with mock email data.

D. Integration & End-to-End Testing
Inter-Module Communication:

Ensure that the Conductor MCP correctly routes emails to the Email MCP.

Validate that high-risk email drafts are routed to the Dashboard.

Pilot Testing:

Run a closed pilot with sample emails to simulate real-world conditions.

Collect feedback and monitor logs for performance and error handling.

Phase 2: Enhancement & Stabilization (Weeks 9-14)
Refinement Based on Feedback:

Iterate on intent analysis algorithms and LLM prompts based on pilot feedback.

Enhance logging, error handling, and security measures.

Dashboard Improvements:

Expand UI features (filtering, search, detailed logs).

Enhance user feedback mechanisms.

Scalability & Performance:

Optimize the API performance between modules.

Scale containerized services to handle increased load.

Security Enhancements:

Secure API endpoints and integrate data encryption.

Set up monitoring for security threats.

Phase 3: Future Expansion & Additional Modules (Post Week 14)
Plan for New Modules:

Document API contracts and integration points for future modules (e.g., Content Generator, Scheduling MCP).

Continuous Improvement:

Monitor system performance and update algorithms based on ongoing user feedback.

Expand functionalities in an agile manner.

3. Best Practices & Key Considerations
Modular Architecture:
Each module (Email MCP, Conductor MCP, Dashboard) is designed as a microservice with clearly defined API interfaces for ease of maintenance and scalability.

Iterative Development:
Use agile sprints to develop, test, and refine the MVP. Regularly incorporate user feedback and adjust workflows accordingly.

Robust Logging & Monitoring:
Implement comprehensive logging at every step. Utilize monitoring tools to track performance, detect issues, and ensure transparency in decision-making.

Security First:
Even in the MVP, ensure that data transmission and storage are secured. Use encryption, secure authentication, and regular security audits.

Human in the Loop:
Emphasize the dashboard’s role in overseeing high-risk decisions. Ensure that human review is integrated early to validate and improve automated decisions.

Scalability and Future Proofing:
Plan for the future by designing the Conductor MCP to easily integrate new modules. Document APIs and maintain a modular design to accommodate additional functionalities later on.

4. Milestones & Deliverables
Week 1-2:

Finalized requirements and system design.

Architecture diagrams and API contracts documented.

Week 3-8 (MVP):

Email MCP: Email parsing, LLM integration, risk assessment, and auto-response functionality.

Conductor MCP: Intent analysis, routing logic, logging, and integration with Email MCP.

Basic Dashboard: UI for monitoring and human feedback.

Successful end-to-end integration test.

Week 9-14 (Enhancements):

Improved intent analysis and LLM prompts.

Enhanced dashboard features.

Performance optimizations and security updates.

Comprehensive pilot testing with real user feedback.

Post Week 14 (Expansion):

Roadmap for additional modules.

Continuous improvement cycles based on operational data and feedback.

5. Summary
By starting with a focused MVP that integrates the Email MCP and Conductor MCP with a basic Dashboard, we can validate core functionalities, establish robust logging, and ensure human oversight. This iterative, modular approach not only addresses immediate needs but also sets the stage for future expansion, enabling us to integrate additional modules as our platform scales. Adhering to best practices in modular architecture, security, and continuous improvement will be key to the project's long-term success.